---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout
  title="Workspaces — Varve Docs"
  description="Varve's six task-focused workspaces — one document, one history, one canvas, with Logo tools in Design and shared Code export."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Workspaces
      </nav>

      <h1 class="site-page-title">Workspaces</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Varve organizes its tools into six task-focused workspaces. Each workspace
        curates the toolbar, panels, and workflow around a specific kind of design
        work — but they all edit the <strong>same document</strong>, so nothing is
        lost when you switch.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>The Six Workspaces</h2>
        <p>
          Switch workspaces from the switcher in the menubar: the active mode is
          always named, every other mode is one icon away, and modes that don't fit
          stay reachable from the switcher's overflow menu. The same commands are
          available in <strong>View &gt; Workspace</strong>, the command palette, and
          the keyboard shortcuts below. Each mode remembers its own default tool,
          panels, and toolbar layout.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Design</strong> (<code>Ctrl+Shift+1</code>) — UI/UX, components, and prototyping. Default tool: Select.</li>
          <li><strong>Print</strong> (<code>Ctrl+Shift+2</code>) — Multi-page layouts, typography, preflight, and colour management. Default tool: Select.</li>
          <li><strong>Draw</strong> (<code>Ctrl+Shift+3</code>) — Raster painting, vector freehand, and brushes. Default tool: Paint.</li>
          <li><strong>Photo</strong> (<code>Ctrl+Shift+4</code>) — Nondestructive photo editing and adjustments. Keeps your current tool; the toolbar leads with Select, Lasso, and the retouch and mask flyouts.</li>
          <li><strong>Motion</strong> (<code>Ctrl+Shift+5</code>) — Timeline animation, keyframes, and easing. Default tool: Select.</li>
          <li><strong>Email</strong> (<code>Ctrl+Shift+6</code>) — Email-specific authoring, responsive preview, compatibility checks, and export. Default tool: Select.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The switcher and workspace shortcuts use the same order: Design, Print, Draw, Photo, Motion, Email on <code>Ctrl+Shift+1</code> through <code>Ctrl+Shift+6</code>. On macOS, use Command+Shift with the same numbers. Logo work stays in Design; <code>Ctrl+Shift+7</code> opens Design and shows Logo Tools. Code export is a shared panel; <code>Ctrl+Shift+8</code> opens it in the current workspace. These final two shortcuts also use Command+Shift on macOS.</p>
        <p>
          Note: the older <code>Ctrl+Shift+D/P/R/I/M</code> bindings shown in some
          early documentation no longer switch workspaces — those keys are now used
          by Repeat Duplicate, Present, Invert Selection, and Preview Mode.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>One Document, Many Modes</h2>
        <p>Workspace switching is deliberately low-cost. All six workspaces share:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One document model</strong> — a mode never forks the scene; there is no "Photo copy" of your file</li>
          <li><strong>State preservation</strong> — document, viewport, and undo history carry across switches; selections and edit targets are revalidated when a surface changes</li>
          <li><strong>Shared fundamentals</strong> — selection, transform, colour, layers, undo/redo, and copy/paste behave identically in every mode</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This means you can sketch in Draw, refine in Design, check print colour in
          Print, and animate in Motion — all in the same session, with one history.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Surface-aware canvas labels</h2>
        <p>
          A Design Canvas is an unbounded editing surface. Its frame and object labels, minimap,
          accessibility tree, hit testing, and selection all use the same resolved occurrence set,
          so content from another canvas is not shown merely because it shares a world coordinate
          or name. The transparent storage root for a canvas is structural and never appears as an
          artwork label.
        </p>
        <p>
          Print follows the publishing pasteboard: page decorations can identify placed pages,
          while Design Canvas artwork remains separate. Labels are editor-only derived UI and are
          excluded from saved scene content and exports. When a user changes surfaces, transient
          selection and edit targets are cleared or revalidated before the new surface is acted on.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Customizing a Workspace</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Customize Workspace…</strong> (View menu or command palette) controls every
          surface a workspace owns, applied instantly and remembered per mode:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Panels</strong> — show or hide Layers, Inspector, Timeline, Email Preview, Email Output, Resources, shared Code, Logo tools, and History.</li>
          <li><strong>Toolbar</strong> — hide tools, including tools that live inside flyouts (boolean operations, retouch, mask), and place the palette at the <strong>top or bottom</strong> of the canvas (View menu). Select, Hand, and Zoom always stay available.</li>
          <li><strong>Toolbar arrangement</strong> — move tools earlier or later, place a tool in the main row or an existing flyout, and pin tools to keep them ahead of responsive overflow. Select, Hand, and Zoom stay in the main row.</li>
          <li><strong>Inspector tabs</strong> and <strong>status bar sections</strong> — per-workspace visibility and ordering; pin inspector tabs to keep them ahead of overflow.</li>
          <li><strong>Editor chrome</strong> — floating toolbar, status bar, and document tabs.</li>
          <li><strong>Panel widths</strong> — resize the Layers, Inspector, and Resources panels with the drag handle; widths are remembered per workspace.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          In Resources &gt; Libraries, import a JSON library, select its row to view details, or
          use the separate Uninstall button. Both row actions work from the keyboard. On desktop,
          the tool palette stays beside the open Resources panel as you resize it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Layouts survive restarts even where browser storage is unreliable, and
          <strong>Show All Panels</strong> (View menu) recovers an accidentally hidden layout.
          Reset returns the current mode to its built-in layout; Reset All asks before clearing
          every workspace's customizations.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Saving Named Layouts</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Manage Layouts…</strong> (View menu or the command palette) saves the
          current arrangement as a named layout and applies it later. Applying a layout
          changes only the chrome — the document, selection, undo history, and active
          workspace mode are untouched.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Templates</strong> — <em>Default</em> restores the mode's built-in arrangement, <em>Every panel</em> reveals every panel, and <em>Focus canvas</em> hides the panels, status bar, and tabs while keeping the floating toolbar.</li>
          <li><strong>Your layouts</strong> — save the current arrangement, update it, rename, duplicate, copy as portable JSON, import JSON, or delete with confirmation. Duplicate names are never silently overwritten.</li>
          <li><strong>Recovery</strong> — resetting a workspace snapshots the arrangement first; Manage Layouts offers Restore until the next reset, so a reset is not a one-way door.</li>
          <li><strong>Portable and private</strong> — exported layouts contain only a name and registered panel/tool arrangement. They never include file paths, machine geometry, or document content, and imported layouts are validated before they are applied.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Detached Panel Windows</h2>
        <p>
          Desktop builds can detach <strong>Layers, Inspector, Assets, Code,</strong>{' '}and{' '}
          <strong>Logo</strong> into individual auxiliary windows. Use a panel's detach button,
          header context menu, or supported header drag gesture. The panel remains connected to
          the same document, selection, and undo history; its window also provides a reattach
          command.
        </p>
        <p>
          Detachment is transactional: the docked panel stays visible until the auxiliary host is
          ready, and a failed or timed-out transfer rolls back to the primary window. Browser
          builds keep panels in the main editor window; they do not open browser popups. Pages,
          Timeline, and History are not currently detachable.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Toolbar Composition</h2>
        <p>
          Each workspace declares its own toolbar: which tools appear, their order,
          group separators, and which tools live in flyouts (for example, the Shapes
          and Boolean-operation flyouts in Design mode). Customizing a workspace —
          hiding a tool or moving it to a flyout — applies through your preference
          overrides and is reflected in the toolbar immediately.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use <strong>Customize Workspace…</strong> to change tool order with Move Up/Move Down,
          assign tools to the main row or a flyout already provided by that workspace, and pin
          selected tools ahead of responsive overflow. The same controls are available for
          inspector-tab and status-section ordering; no drag gesture is required.
        </p>
        <p>
          The palette is responsive. When the canvas column is too narrow for every
          declared tool, it keeps creation and navigation tools in the row and moves
          the lowest-priority tools — inspection, AI helpers, and context-dependent
          commands such as boolean operations — behind the <strong>More tools</strong>
          control at the trailing edge, which reports how many tools it holds. Widening
          the window or resizing the side panels restores them.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Next Steps</h2>
        <p>
          See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/getting-started/interface')}>Interface Overview</a>{' '}
          for the surrounding layout, or the{' '}
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/keyboard-shortcuts')}>keyboard shortcuts reference</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }
  .docs-page h1 { font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144; letter-spacing: -0.015em; }

  .docs-section h2 { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-accent); padding-bottom: var(--space-3); }

</style>
